title: Input/Output Console
manual:

The two consoles are the command-line program inside the GUI. The Input console takes a
critic2 script, with the same keywords as an input file, and runs it on the current
system; the Output console keeps what every run printed. The keywords are documented in
the online manual, and the "Help" button of the Input console gives a reference for each.

# Input console

- "Clear" empties the text; "Template" inserts the template of a critic2 command;
  "Help" brings up the critic2 command reference.
- "System" chooses the current system and "Field" its reference field: the commands are
  applied to them. The system follows the selection in the tree unless the preference
  "Tree selects input console system" is off.
- "RUN" (Ctrl+Enter with the console focused) runs the text as a script. Right-click the
  button for "Run on All Systems", which runs the same commands on every loaded system.

The script runs as it would in the command-line program, with the current system as the
loaded structure and the reference field as the current field. A command that changes the
geometry (EDIT, NEWCELL) or the critical points (AUTO) updates the view and the tree when
it finishes. The CRYSTAL, MOLECULE and RESET keywords are refused in the GUI, where
structures are opened with File > New or File > Open: the script reports it in the output
and goes on with the next line.

# Output console

Every run is a record, headed by "### Command: N" with the time, the system, the field
and the input text, and listed as a numbered button above the text area, newest first.
Click a button to show that record alone, or "All" for the whole log. Hover a button for
its system, field and input, and right-click it for "Edit Input", which copies its text
back to the Input console, and "Remove".

- "Clear" (shown with "All" active) empties the log; "Copy" copies the record on display
  to the clipboard and "Save" writes it to a file; "Remove All" discards every record.
- The tab of the window shows "Output" with a dot when something was written since the
  console was last focused.

The "Please cite" blocks that the methods print in the command-line program (NCI, XDM,
Bader integration and others) appear here too; the same references are in the Help topic
of the corresponding tool window.
